[{"id":"Xs2XTHzAVC9YnafnPyzj","begin":"2026-10-20T06:00:00+00:00","created":"2026-10-20T08:35:37+00:00","end":"2026-10-20T12:08:00+00:00","modified":"2026-10-28T00:38:39+00:00","external_desc":"Multiple services were experiencing issues in the europe-west2 region.","most_recent_update":{"created":"2026-10-27T15:13:38+00:00","modified":"2026-10-28T00:38:39+00:00","when":"2026-10-27T15:13:38+00:00","text":"## Incident Report\n### **Summary**\nOn Monday, 19 Oct 2026 (Tuesday, 20 Oct BST), a data center facility that hosts europe-west2-b, experienced an external electrical power disruption. This resulted in service unavailability for workloads located in this zone. As traffic was redirected to alternative cloud regions or unaffected zones within europe-west2 cloud region, these workloads experienced transient latency and elevated errors affecting services across europe-west2. \nThe primary impact was mitigated after 6 hours 8 minutes.\n### **Root Cause**\n**Facility architecture and background:**\nThe data center facility in the UK region hosts Cloud clusters serving the europe-west2-b zone, which form part of the multi-zonal europe-west2 Cloud region. The site relies on a 2N utility power architecture—meaning the facility has complete duplicate power paths for every critical load—backed by a central high-voltage generator bank managed by a dual-redundant Programmable Logic Controller (PLC).\nPrimary utility power is supplied to the facility via two independent feeders (A and B), supplemented by a standby utility connection configured for manual campus-wide transfer during maintenance operations or emergency events. The two high-voltage primary feeders terminate at separate primary intake switchboards capable of closed-loop operation if either board is taken offline. Switching between the primary and standby utility providers utilizes open transitions and key interlocks to prevent parallel connections across sources.\nStatic Uninterruptible Power Supply (UPS) battery systems provide 5 minutes of autonomous runtime. This bridge power ensures uninterrupted operation while the PLC detects utility undervoltage events, initiates diesel generator startup, synchronizes the generators, and transfers the site load.\nPrior to the utility disruption, the facility had been placed in a temporary single-feed (1N) bridged maintenance posture on the primary utility. Circuit breaker feeder B was physically opened and key-interlocked (Locked Out Tagged Out) to allow for corrective repairs following issues identified during a quarterly generator load test on 14 Oct 2026. Consequently, primary utility feed A was supporting the total critical load while repairs were conducted on the B feeder pathway.\n**Root Cause:**\nOn Monday, 19 Oct 2026 at 22:53 US/Pacific (Tuesday, 20 Oct 06:53 BST), the primary utility provider experienced an outage on primary utility feed A, caused by an external underground cable fault on the utility network outside the data center facility.  \nWhen the primary utility feed A was lost, the facility’s generator plant failed to receive a signal to automatically start and take critical load due to a design voltage-sensing and misconfiguration in the PLCs. The facility's static UPS battery banks carried the critical load as designed until their battery reserves depleted resulting in a complete power loss across data halls.\nThe investigation of the PLC configuration identified the root cause as a misconfiguration of the PLC logic that did not verify both an undervoltage condition and physical breaker position for both utility feeders. Instead, the PLC undervoltage sensing inputs were wired upstream (line side) of the isolated utility feed B breaker. The PLC was programmed to verify whether voltage was present on the feeder before commanding a generator start. As the utility feed B remained energized upstream, both PLCs sensed live upstream voltage on the disconnected path. As a result, the PLC logic indicated that a healthy utility source was available, even though the primary and secondary distribution feeds were de-energized, and did not issue a start command to the generator plant.;\nThe onsite facilities team attempted to execute a remote load transfer via the PLC; however, the system inhibited the action. Recognizing that the UPS battery reserves would be exhausted before further remote operations could be resolved, the team transferred the facility load to the standby utility source, restoring the critical load that was lost once batteries depleted during this process.\nOnce utility power was restored via the standby utility source, and UPS batteries started recharging, the onsite facility teams and OEM vendors reconfigured and validated PLC functionality on both undervoltage and breaker status.\nAfter the power loss was initially detected, Google engineers began work to mitigate failover failures and prepare service recovery. Upon restoration of the utility source, UPS and generator capabilities, they began the process to re-enable services in europe-west2-b.\n### Remediation and Prevention\nThe sequence of events leading to customer impact was as follows:\n* **19 Oct 22:53 US/Pacific (20 Oct BST 06:53 BST) -** An electrical power disruption occurred at the data center facility hosting availability zone europe-west2-b, and a PLC (Programmable Logic Controller) startup fault prevented facility emergency generators from assuming the electrical load. Facility load continued to be supported by UPS battery banks until the batteries were depleted.\n* **19 Oct 23:00 US/Pacific (20 Oct 07:00 BST)** **Impact start -** As battery backups were exhausted, physical host servers, local storage infrastructure, and network aggregation hardware in availability zone europe-west2-b lost power, causing instances, database replicas, and worker nodes in that zone to become immediately unreachable.\n* **19 Oct 23:04 US/Pacific (20 Oct 07:04 BST) -** Google engineers were alerted to the disruption via automated monitoring systems and probers, which detected elevated API error ratios, job submission timeouts, and loss of infrastructure reachability in zone europe-west2-b. Engineering teams immediately mobilized to isolate the affected infrastructure and commence recovery.\n* **19 Oct 23:09 US/Pacific (20 Oct 07:09 BST) -** Regional traffic-routing mechanisms and client applications initiated automated failover toward available infrastructure in adjacent availability zones within the region. * Cross-zone remote procedure calls from regional service control planes directed to metadata and catalog components in the offline zone encountered timeouts, causing initial job submission stalls and API latency spikes. * The sudden influx of redirected query executions, high-availability database failovers, and compute workloads saturated available compute slots and processing capacity in other availability zones, creating elevated admission queues, delayed database failover completions, and intermittent 500 and 503 API errors across the region. * **Traffic Isolation:** Google engineers isolated the offline availability zone (europe-west2-b) by adjusting regional service routing away from the affected infrastructure, preventing cross-zone client requests from timing out against offline endpoints. * **Workload Reprioritization and Resource Safeguards:** Engineers adjusted scheduler allocations and capacity limits across unimpacted infrastructure to prioritize active customer reservations, reduce resource contention on execution slots, and relieve queue backlogs for regional workloads.\n* **19 Oct 23:54 US/Pacific (20 Oct 07:54 BST) Utility Power Restoration** **\\-** Power supply from the standby utility source was restored and power restoration to the data hall began.\n* **20 Oct 00:20 US/Pacific (08:20 BST) Datahall Power Restoration** **\\-** Utility power was re-established to the datahalls. Mains utility power normalized; power feed to data hall verified. Following this, the site operations and engineering teams focused on and completed a reset and tested the emergency backup generator automation controllers to restore facility power.\n* **20 Oct 00:40 US/Pacific (08:40 BST)** **\\-** Clusters began to recover, cluster manager systems started rescheduling compute jobs. \n* **20 Oct 00:45 US/Pacific (08:45 BST) \\-** Network devices were back online. \n* **20 Oct 00:50 US/Pacific (08:50 BST) \\-** Due to the immediacy of power loss, storage devices with unclean shutdowns began recovery. Responders recognized this and chose not to restore services that would otherwise make this worse. Because of the large volume of storage devices involved, this process required a substantial period to finish prior to restoring service to customers.\n* **20 Oct 03:13 US/Pacific (11:06 BST)** **\\-** Power redundancy was verified and the storage infrastructure was sufficiently restored to prepare the initiation of controlled traffic re-admission. The remaining storage systems continued recovery operations.\n* **20 Oct 03:45 US/Pacific (11:45 BST) Controlled Traffic Re-admission** **\\-** Once system health and storage integrity were verified, engineers executed gradual, controlled traffic re-admission back to zone europe-west2-b allowing accumulated job backlogs to clear smoothly.\n* **20 Oct 04:11 US/Pacific (12:11 BST)** **\\-** Google Cloud Platform services and APIs were made fully available to customers. Some Google Compute Engine virtual machines were still not yet available.\n* **20 Oct 04:29 US/Pacific (12:29 BST) \\-** Regional services and backend compute management systems were fully undrained, marking the end of core platform-level disruption.\n* **20 Oct 05:08 US/Pacific (13:08 BST) Majority Workload Recovery** **\\-** GCE virtual machines were 99.5% operational and healthy, and creating new VMs was fully supported. Service availability was restored for most customer workloads, with resolution confirmed for the majority of users across Google Cloud Platform services in the region. Following the recovery of a small tail of remaining isolated workloads, the closure notification for the incident was posted by 07:03 US/Pacific (15:03 BST).\nGoogle is committed to preventing a repeat of this issue in the future and is completing the following actions:\n**Data Center Action Items:**\n* Restoration of normal utility configuration (both A & B feeds) * Utility provider to locate the damaged cable fault, using low-frequency radar penetration testing procedures. **\\[Completed 26 Oct\\]** * Utility provider to send on-site engineers to repair the cable fault and restore the impacted primary utility feed A source. **\\[Estimated Completion Date 30 Oct\\]** * Onsite Operations teams with a controlled method of procedure (MOP) to transfer the facility to its primary utility source (feeds A and B) from standby utility feed through coordinated switching with the utility provider **\\[Estimated Completion Date 31 Oct\\]**\n* Generator confidence testing * Full generator load transfer tests with utility power cutover of the active utility feed. Test automatic startup of generators through PLC signals within the backup battery timer and detailed review of backup procedures **\\[Completed on 20 Oct and 26 Oct (full critical load transfer test including updated EOPs)\\]** * Full generator load transfer tests in coordination with the primary utility source feeds (A and B) after restoration work completed by the primary utility provider. **\\[Estimated Completion Date 31 Oct\\]**\n* PLC configuration fix  * Reconfigured and validated the PLC after the outage on 20 Oct and then again on 26 Oct **\\[Completed 26 Oct\\]** * Google engineers to work in collaboration with the facility vendor teams to perform a comprehensive PLC code review and system resiliency to multiple failure scenarios. **\\[Estimated Completion Date (prior to generator transfer test) estimated 31 Oct\\]** * Validate PLC telemetry once primary utility feed A and B lineup is restored. **\\[Estimated Completion Date 31 Oct\\]** * Validate global PLC configurations at all global sites with similar topologies and PLC manufacturers, to ensure confidence in automatic generator starts. **\\[Estimated Completion Date 15 Nov\\]**\n* Emergency Operating Procedures (EOP) documentation update: * Comprehensive update to the EOP for site’s response to unexpected loss of active utility source. Update includes \\- manual steps to attempt to restore active utility feed, manual restart of generators and manual transfer to other utility sources within the UPS battery run time. **\\[Completed 26 Oct\\]** * Ensure updated EOP is practiced twice per shift by on-site facility team. **\\[Completed 24 Oct and checked with Google DC Operations daily\\]**\n* Strengthen change management:  * Freeze planned maintenance activities at other sites with similar topologies, and implement elevated approvals and reviews for data center configuration changes which are outside their standard designs. **\\[Estimated Completion Date 30 Oct\\]**\n**Cloud Service Action Items:**\n* **Communications Enhancement:** Bolster notification speed by developing an automated alert-to-communication mechanism for location-impacting incidents.\n* **Platform Testing:** Expand platform-wide testing outcomes for infrastructure-level events.\n### **Detailed Description of Impact**\nBetween Monday, 19 Oct 2026, 23:00 US/Pacific (Tuesday, 20 Oct 2026, 07:00 BST) and Tuesday, 20 Oct 2026, 05:08 US/Pacific (13:08 BST), customers utilizing Google Cloud Platform services in the europe-west2 region experienced service disruptions across two consecutive phases, lasting a total duration of 6 hours, 08 minutes.\nDuring the initial phase from 23:00 to 04:29 US/Pacific (07:00 to 12:29 BST) (duration 5 hours, 29 minutes), customers experienced unavailability of zone-scoped resources in products, including:\n* **Compute Engine** \\- VM instances, zonal MIGs, unmanaged instance groups, zonal NEGs, sole-tenant node groups, zonal reservations, target instances, zonal API operations. \n* **Persistent Disk / Hyperdisk** \\- Zonal PD, zonal Hyperdisk, Hyperdisk storage pools, instant snapshots.\n* **GKE \\-** Zonal clusters (control plane), node pool nodes in \\-b, PVs on zonal disks.\n* **Cloud SQL \\-** Non-HA instances, read replicas in \\-b.\n* **AlloyDB \\-** Basic (non-HA) primaries in \\-b.\n* **Bigtable \\-** Single-cluster instances in \\-b.\n* **Other products with affected zonal resources:** Cloud Batch, Cloud Build, Cloud Storage, Cloud Workstations, Composer, Dataproc, Filestore, Managed Lustre, Migrate to VMs, NetApp Volumes, Pub/Sub Lite, Vertex AI Workbench, VMware Engine.\nThe unavailability of those resources scoped to a zone that undergoes disruption is working within the design of a zonally-scoped system. Requests made to these resulted either in a timeout or error code during the disruption. Any workloads and resources hosted entirely within europe-west2-b became unreachable due to this event.\nGoogle Compute Engine's zonal resources such as VMs require customers to provision failover or cross-zone recovery resources if needed, and to set up a recovery mechanism that matches their needs.\nSome failovers were unsuccessful due to capacity constraints and difficulty in replicating the underlying storage mechanism. \nFollowing platform service restoration, some customer workloads running on Compute Engine instances as well as products that rely on Compute Engine experienced a longer recovery. By 04:46 US/Pacific (12:46 BST) new Virtual Machine creation resumed and by 05:08 US/Pacific (13:08 BST) over 99% of Compute Engine Virtual Machines were in a healthy state. Any remaining customers that were experiencing Virtual Machine problems were directed to file a customer support case as per normal procedure.\nWhile the physical power outage was isolated strictly to data center facilities in zone europe-west2-b, some customers running workloads in unimpacted zones (europe-west2-a and europe-west2-c) or across regional architectures also experienced unexpected impact.\nThe primary causes of impact outside of zone europe-west2-b were:\n1\\) Instantaneous demand surges on neighboring zones (a and c) and\n2\\) Control-plane dependencies across zonal boundaries\nAffected products included: AlloyDB, Apigee Infra and Apigee Edge, BigQuery, Cloud Dataflow, Cloud Pub/Sub, Cloud SQL, Google Compute Engine, Google Kubernetes Engine, Looker, Regional Persistent Disk, Vertex AI Prediction, and Cloud Speech-to-Text.\nThese services experienced unexpected degradation and failover issues driven by sudden compute capacity exhaustion from surging demand, and control-plane API timeouts. Customers would have experienced the inability to create, update, or delete some regional resources during this period. This was not a full regional control-plane outage, but certainly a degraded state.\nMulti-zonal and regional services that spanned across the europe-west2 region experienced elevated latency, transient API errors (such as HTTP 500 and 503 errors), and task admission queuing as failover traffic concentrated onto unimpacted regional resources and cross-zone calls to offline components timed out. \nStorage write operations in the region experienced a transient error rate during the initial failover period, and regional query admission queues temporarily peaked before rapidly subsiding as capacity re-balanced.\nWe did not identify any impact of the outage outside of the europe-west2 region. The following Google Cloud Platform services experienced impact within europe-west2:\n* AlloyDB for PostgreSQL\n* Apigee\n* BigQuery Data Transfer Service\n* Cloud Data Fusion\n* Cloud Filestore\n* Cloud Firestore\n* Cloud Load Balancing\n* Cloud Memorystore\n* Cloud NAT\n* Cloud Run\n* Cloud Spanner\n* Cloud Workstations\n* Datastream\n* Dialogflow CX\n* Google App Engine\n* Google BigQuery\n* Google Cloud Bigtable\n* Google Cloud Dataflow\n* Google Cloud Dataproc\n* Google Cloud NetApp Volumes\n* Google Cloud Pub/Sub\n* Google Cloud SQL\n* Google Cloud Storage (GCS)\n* Google Compute Engine (GCE)\n* Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE)\n* Google SecOps (Security Information and Event Management \\[SIEM\\] and Security Orchestration, Automation, and Response \\[SOAR\\])\n* Looker (Google Cloud core)\n* Looker Studio\n* Managed Airflow (Google Cloud Composer)\n* Managed Service for Apache Kafka\n* Memorystore for Memcached\n* Memorystore for Redis\n* Memorystore for Redis Cluster\n* Memorystore for Valkey\n* Network Connectivity Center\n* Persistent Disk\n* Private Service Connect\n* Speech-to-Text\n* Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)\n* VMWare engine\n----------","status":"AVAILABLE","affected_locations":[]},"status_impact":"SERVICE_DISRUPTION","severity":"medium","service_key":"zall","service_name":"Multiple Products","affected_products":[{"title":"AlloyDB for PostgreSQL","id":"fPovtKbaWN9UTepMm3kJ","current_title":"AlloyDB for PostgreSQL"},{"title":"Apigee","id":"9Y13BNFy4fJydvjdsN3X","current_title":"Apigee"},{"title":"BigQuery Data Transfer Service","id":"UdjkJSWTHMj2b11ftW6E","current_title":"BigQuery Data Transfer Service"},{"title":"Cloud Data Fusion","id":"rLKDHeeaBiXTeutF1air","current_title":"Cloud Data Fusion"},{"title":"Cloud Filestore","id":"jog4nyYkquiLeSK5s26q","current_title":"Cloud Filestore"},{"title":"Cloud Firestore","id":"CETSkT92V21G6A1x28me","current_title":"Cloud Firestore"},{"title":"Cloud Load Balancing","id":"ix7u9beT8ivBdjApTif3","current_title":"Cloud Load Balancing"},{"title":"Cloud Memorystore","id":"LGPLu3M5pcUAKU1z6eP3","current_title":"Cloud Memorystore"},{"title":"Cloud NAT","id":"hCNpnTQHkUCCGxJy35Yq","current_title":"Cloud NAT"},{"title":"Cloud Run","id":"9D7d2iNBQWN24zc1VamE","current_title":"Cloud Run"},{"title":"Cloud Spanner","id":"EcNGGUgBtBLrtm4mWvqC","current_title":"Cloud Spanner"},{"title":"Cloud Workstations","id":"5UUXCiH1vfFHXmbDixrB","current_title":"Cloud Workstations"},{"title":"Datastream","id":"ibJgP4CNKnFojHHw8L3s","current_title":"Datastream"},{"title":"Google App Engine","id":"kchyUtnkMHJWaAva8aYc","current_title":"Google App Engine"},{"title":"Google BigQuery","id":"9CcrhHUcFevXPSVaSxkf","current_title":"Google BigQuery"},{"title":"Google Cloud Bigtable","id":"LfZSuE3xdQU46YMFV5fy","current_title":"Google Cloud Bigtable"},{"title":"Google Cloud Dataflow","id":"T9bFoXPqG8w8g1YbWTKY","current_title":"Google Cloud Dataflow"},{"title":"Google Cloud Dataproc","id":"yjXrEg3Yvy26BauMwr69","current_title":"Google Cloud Dataproc"},{"title":"Google Cloud NetApp Volumes","id":"vtzMUyQ4z9CbA1x6z85s","current_title":"Google Cloud NetApp Volumes"},{"title":"Google Cloud Pub/Sub","id":"dFjdLh2v6zuES6t9ADCB","current_title":"Google Cloud Pub/Sub"},{"title":"Google Cloud SQL","id":"hV87iK5DcEXKgWU2kDri","current_title":"Google Cloud SQL"},{"title":"Google Cloud Storage","id":"UwaYoXQ5bHYHG6EdiPB8","current_title":"Google Cloud Storage"},{"title":"Google Compute Engine","id":"L3ggmi3Jy4xJmgodFA9K","current_title":"Google Compute Engine"},{"title":"Google Kubernetes Engine","id":"LCSbT57h59oR4W98NHuz","current_title":"Google Kubernetes Engine"},{"title":"Looker (Google Cloud core)","id":"QEyA1YSaAS3rJS6O8wHp","current_title":"Looker (Google Cloud core)"},{"title":"Google Cloud Composer","id":"YxkG5FfcC42cQmvBCk4j","current_title":"Managed Airflow"},{"title":"Managed Service for Apache Kafka","id":"QMZ3IpyG3Ooxotv7JOKV","current_title":"Managed Service for Apache Kafka"},{"title":"Memorystore for Memcached","id":"paC6vmsvnjCHsBkp4Wva","current_title":"Memorystore for Memcached"},{"title":"Memorystore for Redis","id":"3yFciKa9NQH7pmbnUYUs","current_title":"Memorystore for Redis"},{"title":"Memorystore for Redis Cluster","id":"pAQRwuhqRn7Y1E2we8ds","current_title":"Memorystore for Redis Cluster"},{"title":"Memorystore for Valkey","id":"QUDi3IaNvixryrShGpGw","current_title":"Memorystore for Valkey"},{"title":"Network Connectivity Center","id":"kFBKMAzrhno1We6f7Ydk","current_title":"Network Connectivity Center"},{"title":"Persistent Disk","id":"SzESm2Ux129pjDGKWD68","current_title":"Persistent Disk"},{"title":"Private Service Connect","id":"fbzQRKqPfxZ2DUScMGV2","current_title":"Private Service Connect"},{"title":"Speech-to-Text","id":"5f5oET9B3whnSFHfwy4d","current_title":"Speech-to-Text"},{"title":"Storage Transfer Service","id":"reC3xJSY6Gzc8n9eYmmj","current_title":"Storage Transfer Service"},{"title":"VMWare engine","id":"9H6gWUHvb2ZubeoxzQ1Y","current_title":"VMWare engine"},{"title":"Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)","id":"BSGtCUnz6ZmyajsjgTKv","current_title":"Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)"}],"uri":"incidents/Xs2XTHzAVC9YnafnPyzj"},{"id":"mSFVt6PiP557bjRyYsut","begin":"2026-10-08T11:00:00+00:00","created":"2026-10-08T18:24:18+00:00","end":"2026-10-09T16:46:00+00:00","modified":"2026-10-14T07:24:12+00:00","external_desc":"Google Cloud Storage (GCS) customers experienced elevated latencies and error rates when performing upload or write operations.","most_recent_update":{"created":"2026-10-14T07:15:32+00:00","modified":"2026-10-14T07:17:30+00:00","when":"2026-10-14T07:15:32+00:00","text":"## Incident Report\n## Date/Time of the Issue (All time US/Pacific)\n**Incident First Occurrence**\nIncident Start: 08 October 2026 04:00\nIncident End: 08 October 2026 16:30\nDuration: 12 hours, 30 minutes\n**Incident Recurrence**\nRecurrence Start: 09 October 2026 04:05\nRecurrence End: 09 October 2026 09:46\nDuration: 5 hours, 41 minutes\n**Total Duration:** 18 hours, 11 minutes\n## Summary\nOn Thursday, 08 October 2026, Google Cloud Storage experienced elevated write latency and write error rates while performing upload or write operations in the us-central1 region for a duration of 12 hours, 30 minutes. This was followed by a recurrence on Friday, 09 October 2026 in the us-central1-b zone for a duration of 5 hours, 41 minutes. We are taking immediate steps to ensure this doesn’t happen again.\n## Root Cause\nGoogle Cloud Storage relies on a highly distributed backend architecture to process and store incoming data. During the incident window, specific storage backend nodes within the us-central1 region experienced a localized capacity shortfall due to increased traffic.\nThe sequence of events leading to customer impact was as follows:\n* A traffic sorting optimization configuration update to the Google Cloud Storage load balancing layer intended to address localized capacity constraints caused severe resource exhaustion on specific storage backend nodes in the affected region by inadvertently bypassing the standard capacity-aware routing logic, resulting in a severe load imbalance.\n* Consequently, a small fraction of storage backend nodes in the us-central1 region received an overwhelming proportion of incoming write traffic despite having low available serving capacity. This concentration of traffic led to resource exhaustion on the affected backend servers causing backend services handling object data ingestion to begin timing out.\n* Because the underlying instances could not process the high volume of requests quickly enough, the incoming upload and write requests routed to these nodes experienced elevated latency and failed to complete, resulting in errors surfaced to customers.\n* Concurrently, a separate issue with the underlying database service contributed to additional metadata processing errors during a portion of the incident window.\n* The recurrence of the issue on October 9 was caused by an incomplete rollback of an upload planner feature, which broke the load balancing mechanism and overloaded a few smaller storage backend nodes.\n## Remediation and Prevention\nThe issue was initially detected on 08 October 2026 at 04:50 by Google engineering teams actively monitoring system telemetry early in the incident window. \nTo mitigate the issue, Google engineers executed the following step-by-step strategy:\n* Engineers initially attempted to dynamically allocate additional emergency capacity to the affected region, but this was blocked by systemic regional capacity limits.\n* To relieve immediate pressure, engineers selectively drained traffic from the most overloaded storage backend nodes.\n* A configuration change was deployed to disable the traffic sorting optimization, significantly reducing resource overhead on the storage backend.\n* For the concurrent metadata issue, engineers rolled back a recent update to the underlying metadata database service.\n* While these steps provided temporary relief, a recurrence of the load imbalance required further traffic redirection and draining of affected storage components. The incident was permanently resolved when engineering fully reverted the problematic load balancing configuration change, restoring the previous capacity-aware routing behavior and allowing traffic to distribute evenly across the region.\nOnce traffic levels stabilized and resource utilization returned to normal baseline levels, the previously drained nodes were restored to active service, successfully restoring normal operations.\n**Preventative Actions:**\nTo ensure this issue does not recur, we are implementing the following improvements:\n- **Safe Configuration Rollouts:** We are updating our deployment protocols to ensure that load balancing logic changes are rigorously validated in alternative, smaller regions before being rolled out to larger, highly utilized regions.\n- **Intelligent Operational Controls:** We are enhancing the storage load balancing architecture to strictly enforce capacity constraints, ensuring that traffic distribution logic cannot bypass pre-spilling safeguards and route traffic to clusters lacking sufficient serving resources.\n- **Resilient Scaling Logic:** We are implementing targeted improvements to accelerate internal data movement within highly utilized storage cells, enabling the system to process data more efficiently and handle temporary load spikes with greater resilience.\n## Description of Impact\nOn 08 October 2026, from 04:00 to 16:30 in us-central1 region and 09 October 2026, from 04:05 to 09:46 in us-central1-b zone, customers experienced elevated latency and error rates during Cloud Storage upload and write operations.\n* **Upload & Write Failures:** Customers primarily observed HTTP 503 UNAVAILABLE errors on WriteObject operations and client-side timeouts.\n* **Increased Write Latency:** Write operations that did succeed experienced significantly higher latencies than normal.\n* Cloud Storage operations in all other regions, as well as all other Google Cloud services, remained unaffected by this specific event.","status":"AVAILABLE","affected_locations":[]},"status_impact":"SERVICE_DISRUPTION","severity":"medium","service_key":"UwaYoXQ5bHYHG6EdiPB8","service_name":"Google Cloud Storage","affected_products":[{"title":"Google Cloud Storage","id":"UwaYoXQ5bHYHG6EdiPB8","current_title":"Google Cloud Storage"}],"uri":"incidents/mSFVt6PiP557bjRyYsut"},{"id":"41E5S3mkTGDfkZuJZH5k","begin":"2026-02-27T12:37:00+00:00","created":"2026-02-27T16:12:30+00:00","end":null,"modified":"2026-03-09T05:25:43+00:00","external_desc":"Vertex AI Gemini API customers experienced increased error rates when accessing the global endpoint.","most_recent_update":{"created":"2026-03-09T05:25:43+00:00","modified":"2026-03-09T05:25:43+00:00","when":"2026-03-09T05:25:43+00:00","text":"# Incident Report\n## Summary\nOn Friday, 27 February 2026 at 04:37 US/Pacific, customers using Vertex AI Gemini API models experienced increased error rates. Impacted services included Google Cloud Support, Agent Assist, Vertex Gemini API and Dialogflow CX in US regions and the global endpoint. The issue persisted for a duration of 1 hour and 58 minutes.\nThis is not the level of quality and reliability we strive to offer you, and we have taken immediate steps to improve the platform’s performance and availability.\n## Root Cause\nThis incident was caused by a configuration change to a safety filtering service that supports all Gemini models. For some specific requests, this created code paths that eventually led to service disruptions and capacity loss for the safety filtering service. Consequently, customers encountered overload (429 and 503) errors for their queries, with some users reporting elevated error rates for specific models in US regions.\n## Remediation and Prevention\nGoogle engineers were alerted to the issue via our automated monitoring system on Friday, 27 February 2026 04:54 US/Pacific and immediately started an investigation.\nEngineers identified the faulty configuration change and initiated a rollback to restore the previous stable configuration. Engineers also added more capacity to the service to stabilize it. Full service restoration was confirmed by 06:35 US/Pacific as the rollback propagated and servers became healthy. \\ \\\nGoogle is committed to preventing a repeat of this issue and is taking the following actions:\n* Reinforcing rollout processes to include mandatory validation checkpoints.\n* Improving alerting systems to monitor critical dependencies more closely.\n## ## Detailed Description of Impact\nOn Friday, 27 February 2026 between 04:37 and 06:35 US/Pacific, customers accessing Vertex Gemini APIs may have experienced the following:\n* **Affected Models:** All Vertex AI Gemini API models were affected, including gemini-2.5-flash, gemini-2.5-flash-lite, gemini-2.5-pro, gemini-3.0-flash-preview, gemini-3.0-pro-preview, gemini-2.0-flash, gemini-2.0-flash-lite.\n* **Error Experience:** * **PayGo Customers:** Experienced primarily 429 Resource Exhausted errors. * **Provisioned Throughput (PT) Customers:** Received 503 Service Unavailable errors. * For PT customers, most errors stopped at **06:00**. For PayGo customers, most errors stopped at **06:20**.\n* **Geographic Scope:** Global endpoint, us-central1, us-east4, and other US regions were impacted.","status":"AVAILABLE","affected_locations":[]},"status_impact":"SERVICE_INFORMATION","severity":"low","service_key":"zall","service_name":"Multiple Products","affected_products":[{"title":"Agent Assist","id":"eUntUKqUrHdbBLNcVVXq","current_title":"Agent Assist"},{"title":"Dialogflow CX","id":"BnCicQdHSdxaCv8Ya6Vm","current_title":"Dialogflow CX"},{"title":"Vertex Gemini API","id":"Z0FZJAMvEB4j3NbCJs6B","current_title":"Gemini on Agent Platform"},{"title":"Google Cloud Support","id":"bGThzF7oEGP5jcuDdMuk","current_title":"Google Cloud Support"}],"uri":"incidents/41E5S3mkTGDfkZuJZH5k"}]
